    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80225.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 85,
    "turn_text": "Well I think when I came here I totally opposed it because in California the Constitution prohibits multi-issue legislation. You can't do a fishing game code and a tax code and a welfare code and a water code on one bill in California. You couldn't Washington. I thought that was wrong because it kind of made it very confusing having been here now. I don't think we would get anything done. unless you could attach those amendments. And the reason is very few bills, and this is really important to learn, very few bills get to the president. So all of these issues that are so important to the country, and there's a lot of good stuff, too, that gets into those bills. I'll give you an example that my predecessor in this job was Leon Panetta. uh... he'd work for some time with local government creating a uh... national ring thanks were in and carried the bill through a democratic congress but uh... with bill couldn't get out on its own it just wasn't uh... you know important after long came the uh... bailout for the hurricane in florida with hurricane mitch and uh... he just attached his bill that is a writer and it and it was you know it was the president that was going to find that bill That was President Bush. So one of the things you're looking for is a bill that the President must sign, and a bill that will get to the President's desk. And when you see that opportunity, everybody tries to pile on.",
